\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D4}{\ignorespaces Head analysis by corpus and size (means over ten seeds). Candidates were the $k$ heads with the highest product of prefix-matching and copying scores; in grouped models they were in layer 0 in all but one model (a 3.4M stable-kind model chose one layer-1 head), and in random-order models a few were in later layers. In the 3.4M and 10.9M models layer 0 has exactly $k$ non-candidate heads, so the random draws repeat one set. Prefix: highest prefix-matching score of any head (uniform attention gives about 0.027). Copy score of unseen nouns and of trained kinds intact, with the candidates mean-ablated, and with random heads ablated (mean of 200 or 20 draws). DLA: change in unseen copy score predicted by the candidates' direct paths. In stable-kind 10.9M models, ablating the candidates raised unseen nouns' copying (2.98 to 5.80); we have no explanation for this.}}{18}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:M4}{{D4}{18}{Head analysis by corpus and size (means over ten seeds). Candidates were the $k$ heads with the highest product of prefix-matching and copying scores; in grouped models they were in layer 0 in all but one model (a 3.4M stable-kind model chose one layer-1 head), and in random-order models a few were in later layers. In the 3.4M and 10.9M models layer 0 has exactly $k$ non-candidate heads, so the random draws repeat one set. Prefix: highest prefix-matching score of any head (uniform attention gives about 0.027). Copy score of unseen nouns and of trained kinds intact, with the candidates mean-ablated, and with random heads ablated (mean of 200 or 20 draws). DLA: change in unseen copy score predicted by the candidates' direct paths. In stable-kind 10.9M models, ablating the candidates raised unseen nouns' copying (2.98 to 5.80); we have no explanation for this}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D1}{\ignorespaces Experiment 1. Left: copy score intact, with the candidate heads mean-ablated, and with random heads ablated (nats; mean over ten seeds and three sizes, 95\% intervals over seeds). Right: the candidates' attention from the position that predicts the shape to the context's shape word, stable-kind models (exploratory).}}{19}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:heads}{{D1}{19}{Experiment 1. Left: copy score intact, with the candidate heads mean-ablated, and with random heads ablated (nats; mean over ten seeds and three sizes, 95\% intervals over seeds). Right: the candidates' attention from the position that predicts the shape to the context's shape word, stable-kind models (exploratory)}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {E}Experiment 2, the Embedding Analysis and the Pretrained Test (Amendment 10)}{20}{appendix.E}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:N}{{E}{20}{Experiment 2, the Embedding Analysis and the Pretrained Test (Amendment 10)}{appendix.E}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E1}{\ignorespaces Registered tests of Experiment 2 (stable-kind models; 100 and 1,000 exposures pooled; seed-level estimates over ten seeds, sizes averaged within seed; nats). Holm $p$ is adjusted over H40 to H44. Sizes: number of the three sizes with the estimate's direction. Readings follow the registered table: ``above'' means the Holm threshold was met and the direction held at two of three sizes; TOST $p$ for H40, H41 and H44 was above .99, so none was equivalent.}}{20}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N1}{{E1}{20}{Registered tests of Experiment 2 (stable-kind models; 100 and 1,000 exposures pooled; seed-level estimates over ten seeds, sizes averaged within seed; nats). Holm $p$ is adjusted over H40 to H44. Sizes: number of the three sizes with the estimate's direction. Readings follow the registered table: ``above'' means the Holm threshold was met and the direction held at two of three sizes; TOST $p$ for H40, H41 and H44 was above .99, so none was equivalent}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E2}{\ignorespaces Copy score (nats) by cell, mean of seed values (sizes averaged) with 95\% intervals over ten seeds. Unseen nouns have no exposures; the typical embedding replaces an unseen noun's input embedding with one typical of trained nouns.}}{21}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N2}{{E2}{21}{Copy score (nats) by cell, mean of seed values (sizes averaged) with 95\% intervals over ten seeds. Unseen nouns have no exposures; the typical embedding replaces an unseen noun's input embedding with one typical of trained nouns}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E3}{\ignorespaces Stored preference of the noun's own shape and expectation of a shape rather than a colour at the query position (slot: log of the summed probability of the ten shapes minus that of the ten colours), stable-kind models, mean of seed values (exploratory). Unseen nouns: stored preference \textminus {}0.04, slot 1.49.}}{21}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N3}{{E3}{21}{Stored preference of the noun's own shape and expectation of a shape rather than a colour at the query position (slot: log of the summed probability of the ten shapes minus that of the ten colours), stable-kind models, mean of seed values (exploratory). Unseen nouns: stored preference \textminus {}0.04, slot 1.49}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E4}{\ignorespaces Experiment 2 embedding geometry, stable-kind corpora, 1,000 exposures (unseen nouns: no exposures). Copy scores in nats; cosine of the embedding with its initial value; first-block attention from the query to the context's shape. Seed-level means. Swapping embeddings between changing- and fixed-shape bursts moved the copying with the embedding (2.88 nats [2.01, 3.76] and 2.88 [1.99, 3.76] in the two directions; since all other weights are shared, this mainly confirms that the difference enters through the embedding). In a noun-level regression of the copy score on embedding geometry (cosine with the initial embedding, distance from the trained kinds' mean, norm, size and seed; standard errors clustered by seed), changing-shape bursts scored 3.19 nats [2.46, 3.91] more than geometry predicts at 1,000 exposures and $-$0.09 [$-$0.51, 0.33] at 100. With the per-model regression registered for Experiment~3 (coefficient averaged over sizes within seed; Table~\ref {tab:key}), 2.84 [1.97, 3.70] at 1,000 and 0.15 [$-$0.29, 0.58] at 100.}}{21}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N13}{{E4}{21}{Experiment 2 embedding geometry, stable-kind corpora, 1,000 exposures (unseen nouns: no exposures). Copy scores in nats; cosine of the embedding with its initial value; first-block attention from the query to the context's shape. Seed-level means. Swapping embeddings between changing- and fixed-shape bursts moved the copying with the embedding (2.88 nats [2.01, 3.76] and 2.88 [1.99, 3.76] in the two directions; since all other weights are shared, this mainly confirms that the difference enters through the embedding). In a noun-level regression of the copy score on embedding geometry (cosine with the initial embedding, distance from the trained kinds' mean, norm, size and seed; standard errors clustered by seed), changing-shape bursts scored 3.19 nats [2.46, 3.91] more than geometry predicts at 1,000 exposures and $-$0.09 [$-$0.51, 0.33] at 100. With the per-model regression registered for Experiment~3 (coefficient averaged over sizes within seed; Table~\ref {tab:key}), 2.84 [1.97, 3.70] at 1,000 and 0.15 [$-$0.29, 0.58] at 100}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E5}{\ignorespaces Crossed histories (exploratory), stable-kind corpora, 1,000 exposures (unseen nouns: none): copy score when the context sentence is about a noun of one history (rows) and the query names a different noun of another (columns); 24 items per pair and model, seed-level means. Averaged over familiar histories, a queried noun with changing-shape bursts copied 1.35 nats [0.97, 1.74] more than one with fixed-shape bursts, and a context noun with changing-shape bursts raised copying by 0.39 [0.19, 0.58]. Full results are in the supplementary material.}}{22}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N14}{{E5}{22}{Crossed histories (exploratory), stable-kind corpora, 1,000 exposures (unseen nouns: none): copy score when the context sentence is about a noun of one history (rows) and the query names a different noun of another (columns); 24 items per pair and model, seed-level means. Averaged over familiar histories, a queried noun with changing-shape bursts copied 1.35 nats [0.97, 1.74] more than one with fixed-shape bursts, and a context noun with changing-shape bursts raised copying by 0.39 [0.19, 0.58]. Full results are in the supplementary material}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E6}{\ignorespaces The embedding analysis: copy score (nats) by input embedding, mean over seeds (sizes averaged). H49 (registered, stable-kind): own direction at trained norm minus own \textminus {}0.52 [\textminus {}0.85, \textminus {}0.20], one-sided $p$ = .003, direction at 2 of 3 sizes, supported. In shifting-kind models the same contrast went the other way (0.27 [0.12, 0.41]; exploratory). Random minus own direction, both at trained norm: 0.09 (stable-kind), 0.03 (shifting-kind).}}{22}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N5}{{E6}{22}{The embedding analysis: copy score (nats) by input embedding, mean over seeds (sizes averaged). H49 (registered, stable-kind): own direction at trained norm minus own \textminus {}0.52 [\textminus {}0.85, \textminus {}0.20], one-sided $p$ = .003, direction at 2 of 3 sizes, supported. In shifting-kind models the same contrast went the other way (0.27 [0.12, 0.41]; exploratory). Random minus own direction, both at trained norm: 0.09 (stable-kind), 0.03 (shifting-kind)}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E7}{\ignorespaces Registered tests in the pretrained models, by primary model (nouns or pairs as units; Holm within model over H45 to H48). H45: random (untrained scale) minus original; H46: random (untrained scale) minus typical embedding; H47: colour-typical minus colour-variable nouns, original embeddings; H48: difference between groups in the H45 increase (TOST against $\pm $0.30). A hypothesis needed Holm in at least four of the five models. Outcome: H45 and H46 met Holm in two of five models, H47 in none, and H48 was inconclusive (equivalent in one model).}}{23}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N6}{{E7}{23}{Registered tests in the pretrained models, by primary model (nouns or pairs as units; Holm within model over H45 to H48). H45: random (untrained scale) minus original; H46: random (untrained scale) minus typical embedding; H47: colour-typical minus colour-variable nouns, original embeddings; H48: difference between groups in the H45 increase (TOST against $\pm $0.30). A hypothesis needed Holm in at least four of the five models. Outcome: H45 and H46 met Holm in two of five models, H47 in none, and H48 was inconclusive (equivalent in one model)}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E8}{\ignorespaces Pretrained test: copy score of the context's colour (nats; mean over nouns) by model and input embedding, and with original embeddings by group. Random, own scale: the same random direction at the noun's own norm (exploratory). 70M and 160M are descriptive. Prior entropy: entropy of the colour distribution after \emph  {The \textit  {noun} was} with no context (nats; uniform over eleven colours is 2.40).}}{23}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N7}{{E8}{23}{Pretrained test: copy score of the context's colour (nats; mean over nouns) by model and input embedding, and with original embeddings by group. Random, own scale: the same random direction at the noun's own norm (exploratory). 70M and 160M are descriptive. Prior entropy: entropy of the colour distribution after \emph {The \textit {noun} was} with no context (nats; uniform over eleven colours is 2.40)}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E9}{\ignorespaces Checks of the pretrained test (exploratory). Stored colour: with no context, log probability of a colour-typical noun's coded colour minus the mean over the other colours after \emph  {The \textit  {noun} was} (nats; mean over 22 nouns, and the share of nouns above zero). TOST: equivalence of the H45 and H46 differences within $\pm $0.30 nats over nouns. Norm only: random (own scale) minus random (untrained scale) (same direction, different norm).}}{23}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:N12}{{E9}{23}{Checks of the pretrained test (exploratory). Stored colour: with no context, log probability of a colour-typical noun's coded colour minus the mean over the other colours after \emph {The \textit {noun} was} (nats; mean over 22 nouns, and the share of nouns above zero). TOST: equivalence of the H45 and H46 differences within $\pm $0.30 nats over nouns. Norm only: random (own scale) minus random (untrained scale) (same direction, different norm)}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E1}{\ignorespaces Experiment 2: copy score by history (colour) and presentation (solid: bursts; dashed: spaced) at 100 and 1,000 exposures, in stable-kind (left, pre-registered tests) and shifting-kind (right, exploratory) corpora (means over ten seeds, sizes averaged; 95\% intervals). Solid line and band: unseen nouns; dashed line: unseen nouns with a typical embedding.}}{24}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:a10}{{E1}{24}{Experiment 2: copy score by history (colour) and presentation (solid: bursts; dashed: spaced) at 100 and 1,000 exposures, in stable-kind (left, pre-registered tests) and shifting-kind (right, exploratory) corpora (means over ten seeds, sizes averaged; 95\% intervals). Solid line and band: unseen nouns; dashed line: unseen nouns with a typical embedding}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E2}{\ignorespaces Copy score for colour of real nouns in Pythia models, with their own input embeddings or replaced ones (means over 44 nouns, 95\% intervals). The registered tests used the 1B to 12B models; 70M and 160M are descriptive.}}{24}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pythia}{{E2}{24}{Copy score for colour of real nouns in Pythia models, with their own input embeddings or replaced ones (means over 44 nouns, 95\% intervals). The registered tests used the 1B to 12B models; 70M and 160M are descriptive}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E3}{\ignorespaces Experiment 2. Copy score over training for nouns with 1,000 exposures, by history and presentation, and for unseen nouns (exploratory; saved weights every 1,000 steps; means over ten seeds, sizes averaged; 95\% intervals). A noun with 1,000 exposures has had about 1,000 $\times $ ($t - 0.05$)/0.9 sentences by fraction $t$ of training, so exposure and training time rise together. The unseen-noun line zig-zags by 1 to 2 nats because the three sizes save weights at different fractions of training and differ in level.}}{25}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:a10formation}{{E3}{25}{Experiment 2. Copy score over training for nouns with 1,000 exposures, by history and presentation, and for unseen nouns (exploratory; saved weights every 1,000 steps; means over ten seeds, sizes averaged; 95\% intervals). A noun with 1,000 exposures has had about 1,000 $\times $ ($t - 0.05$)/0.9 sentences by fraction $t$ of training, so exposure and training time rise together. The unseen-noun line zig-zags by 1 to 2 nats because the three sizes save weights at different fractions of training and differ in level}{figure.3}{}}
